  "reputation": {
    "summary": "Billed in 1989 as 'the lowest noise audio operational amplifier available'. The 1990s rec.audio FAQ praised the LT1028 and LT1115 for phono, head amps and mic preamps. diyAudio calls it 'very interesting sounding'; Japanese hobbyists call it the most monitor-like LT part. A srad.jp commenter notes AD797-level noise at half the price. No ASR or Samuel Groner measurements found.",
    "sonic_descriptors": [
      "very interesting sounding",
      "monitor-like",
      "exceptional resolution",
      "clean and quiet"
    ],
    "typical_uses": [
      "MC/MM phono RIAA equaliser / preamp",
      "Moving-coil head amp",
      "Microphone preamplifier",
      "Low-noise measurement preamp with low source impedance",
      "Single-op-amp socket rolling in DIY gear (Audiokarma, diyAudio)"
    ],
    "caveats": [
      "Single only; dual sockets need an adapter.",
      "Only SMD is the 16-lead wide SOIC with odd pinout; no SO-8; tin-lead LT1115CSW obsolete.",
      "Expensive: about 20 USD (Frieve) versus about 2.7 USD for the similar-noise ADA4898-1.",
      "Optimised for low source impedance; with high-impedance sources the 1.2 pA/√Hz current noise erases the benefit.",
      "rec.audio FAQ says 'not unity gain stable'; macromodel says internally compensated. 40 MHz GBW needs careful layout."
    ],
    "sources": [
